SCENARIO-You are collaborating on a college project website with three teammates. Just before a
         client demo, one teammate pushed incomplete code directly to the main branch,
         breaking the layout for everyone else on the team.

Question: Describe the Git workflow your team should adopt to prevent this from happening
          again. Name at least two Git commands your team should use as part of this workflow and
          explain the role of each command.
Answer
      ->Recommended Git Workflow
      -Create a separate branch for each new feature or task.
      Example: feature-login
      
      This keeps unfinished code separate from the stable main branch.
      Make changes and commit regularly on the feature branch.
      Test the code before sharing it.

      Push the branch to the remote repository.
      Other team members can review the changes.
      
      Create a Pull Request (PR) to merge the feature branch into main.
      At least one teammate should review and approve the code.
      
      Merge only after testing and approval.
      The main branch should always contain stable, working code.
      
      Protect the main branch so team members cannot directly push to it.
      
      Important Git Commands
        1. git checkout -b feature-login
           Creates a new branch and switches to it. Developers can work on their feature without affecting main.
      
        2. git add .
           Stages the changed files so they are ready to be committed.
      
        3. git commit -m "Add login page"
           Saves the changes in Git with a meaningful message.
      
        4. git push origin feature-login
           Uploads the feature branch to the remote repository so teammates can review it and create a Pull Request.
      
     Simple Workflow
      main → create feature branch → make changes → commit → push → Pull Request → code review → test → merge into main
